spec:
  grid: 9-16 rows x 9-16 cols, always square [observed]
  background: 7
  maroon_markers:
    color: 9
    components: 1-3 separate 4-connected components
    total_area: 1-17 pixels [observed]
    size_per_component: 1-9 pixels; shapes vary (single pixel, line, L, plus/cross, 2x2 block)
    placement: at least 1 cell from grid edge; typically 2+ cells interior
    sides_with_objects: 0-4 cardinal sides may have an adjacent object (0 = decorative)
  moving_objects:
    count: 3-7 per grid, each a 4-connected monochromatic component
    colors: each object has a unique color, not 7 (bg) or 9 (maroon)
    size: 1-19 pixels; majority 1-5 [observed]
    shape: arbitrary 4-connected polyomino
    maroon_contact: each object is 4-adjacent to exactly one maroon component on exactly one cardinal side
    adjacency_width: typically 1-2 maroon cells touched per object
  push_rule: each object translates away from its maroon contact side until its leading edge hits the grid boundary
  push_distance: 0-8 cells; median ~3; dist>=1 for 95% of objects
  no_collision: moved objects must not overlap each other or maroon in output
  swept_path: object movement from input to output is unobstructed; every intermediate translated position crosses only background outside the object's own cells [observed]
  output: same grid with objects at pushed positions; maroon unchanged; original positions cleared to bg

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size N (9-16), create NxN grid filled with bg=7.
    2. Place 1-3 maroon (9) components in the interior (>=1 cell from edge).
       Each is a small 4-connected polyomino (1-9 cells); keep components 4-separated
       so they do not merge, and keep total maroon area <=17.
    3. For each maroon component, pick 2-4 cardinal sides to attach objects.
       For each chosen side, pick an unused non-bg non-maroon color.
    4. For each (maroon_comp, side) pair, generate a small polyomino (1-5 cells)
       and place it at the grid edge along that side's direction (this is the OUTPUT position).
       Ensure no overlap with existing content.
    5. Derive input: for each object at its edge position, compute reverse translation
       (toward its maroon component) until the object is 4-adjacent to the maroon on
       the correct side. Place object there and clear the edge position.
    6. Validate: each object touches maroon on exactly one side; no overlaps in input or output;
       each object's push distance is 0-8 with zero-distance moves rare; each object's
       swept movement path is clear of maroon and other objects.
